## Summary

I implemented disposable-domain email rejection in the owner-create flow.

**Change** — `OwnerRestControllerV1.java` (the single `POST /api/owners` handler):

1. Added a blocklist constant:
   ```java
   private static final java.util.Set<String> DISPOSABLE_EMAIL_DOMAINS =
       java.util.Set.of("mailinator.com", "tempmail.com", "guerrillamail.com");
   ```

2. Extended `normalizeEmail(...)` — which already runs during create — so that after the existing syntactic validation and lower-casing, it extracts the domain (the part after `@`) and rejects it if present on the blocklist:
   ```java
   String domain = normalized.substring(normalized.indexOf('@') + 1);
   if (DISPOSABLE_EMAIL_DOMAINS.contains(domain)) {
       throw new InvalidOwnerFieldsException(List.of("email"));
   }
   ```

I reused the existing `InvalidOwnerFieldsException`, whose handler in `ExceptionControllerAdvice` already returns **400 Bad Request** (listing `email` in the `errors` array) — consistent with how other invalid-field business rules are handled. The domain comparison is case-insensitive since it's done against the already-lower-cased value. I also updated the method's Javadoc to document the new rule.

The new `AcceptanceTest.errorRejectsDisposableDomain` (posting `x@mailinator.com` and expecting 400) passes, as does the full suite.

